From: Firmware Security Maintenance <security@local.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] bpf: reject local storage deletion from NMI

The retained bpf_selem_unlink(selem, reuse_now) is a void no-fail primitive.
Keep that API and its map teardown caller unchanged. Its four fallible
helper/syscall callers are the sk, inode, task and cgroup deletion functions;
reject NMI there after the existing lookup/busy checks and before unlink.
All current false-reuse unlink callers are covered. Owner destruction uses
bpf_local_storage_destroy directly; map destruction uses reuse_now=true.
Both teardown paths remain unconditionally effective.

As upstream documents, this prevents NMI deferred-free deadlocks only.
Reentrant non-NMI deferred freeing remains outside this partial mitigation.

CVE: CVE-2026-53106
Upstream-Status: Backport [https://git.kernel.org/linus/350de5b8a9befaa2a68861c51f671d4f5f751ca5]
Stable-Commit: e84acaf936970b5b0be2c93bbf255295ba9406df
Stable-Downloaded-Patch-SHA256: b92854468e476992b46cfb36eeb01c9fcd10fc5285eaede3e0e2d7585448ff68

---
--- a/net/core/bpf_sk_storage.c
+++ b/net/core/bpf_sk_storage.c
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
 /* Copyright (c) 2019 Facebook  */
+#include <linux/preempt.h>
 #include <linux/rculist.h>
 #include <linux/list.h>
 #include <linux/hash.h>
@@ -39,6 +40,10 @@
 	sdata = bpf_sk_storage_lookup(sk, map, false);
 	if (!sdata)
 		return -ENOENT;
+
+	/* RCU deferred free is not safe from NMI context. */
+	if (in_nmi())
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 
 	bpf_selem_unlink(SELEM(sdata), false);
 
--- a/kernel/bpf/bpf_inode_storage.c
+++ b/kernel/bpf/bpf_inode_storage.c
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
